Don't read past the end of a UNC path with a host but no share.
unc_root_len() skipped the separator after the host name without
checking that there was one. For a path such as "//host", which has no
share, it stepped over the terminator and scanned whatever followed in
memory, so the result depended on its contents: basename //host could
print "host" as an applet but "//host" when run in the shell, and cd
//tmp could fail although cd //tmp/ succeeded.

Return 0 when nothing follows the host, as already happens for
"//host/".

---
 win32/mingw.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/win32/mingw.c
+++ b/win32/mingw.c
@@ -2480,7 +2480,7 @@
 	if (!is_unc_path(dir))
 		return 0;
 	len = strcspn(s, "/\\");
-	if (len == 0)
+	if (len == 0 || s[len] == '\0')
 		return 0;
 	s += len + 1;
 	len = strcspn(s, "/\\");
